# Signed fixtures are BYTES, not text.
#
# A signature covers the exact bytes it was made over, so any normalisation git applies on
# checkout — CRLF<->LF being the one that fires on Windows — produces a file that no longer
# verifies. The failure is nasty in the specific way it presents: the machine that generated the
# fixture still passes, and only a fresh clone fails, which reads as a broken verifier rather than
# a mangled input.
src-tauri/tests/interop/* -text
